introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How can a problem solve itself?"
  overview: "This unit focuses on <strong>Self-Reference</strong> and <strong>Decomposition</strong>. You will learn how to write functions that call themselves to solve complex problems like sorting (Quicksort) and tree traversal. You will start by tracing the <strong>Runtime Stack</strong> and finish by mastering the <strong>Divide and Conquer</strong> strategy."
  journey: "You'll begin with the anatomy of a recursive method (Base Case vs General Case). Then you'll visualize memory using the <strong>Call Stack</strong> (Winding/Unwinding), and apply this logic to implement efficient algorithms like <strong>Quicksort</strong>."

concepts:
  - title: "The Anatomy of Recursion"
    icon: "fa-recycle"
    content: |
      <p>A recursive method has two parts. Without both, it fails:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Base Case:</strong> The stopping condition (e.g., if n == 0). Stops the infinite loop.</li>
          <li><strong>General (Recursive) Case:</strong> The logic that calls the method again with a smaller input (e.g., return n + Sum(n-1)).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "The Call Stack (Memory)"
    icon: "fa-layer-group"
    content: |
      <p>How does the computer remember where it was? It uses a Stack.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Winding (Push):</strong> Adding a new "Frame" to the stack for each call.</li>
          <li><strong>Unwinding (Pop):</strong> When the Base Case is hit, frames pop off, returning values back up the chain.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Divide & Conquer (Sorting)"
    icon: "fa-sort"
    content: |
      <p>Recursion is powerful for breaking big problems into small ones.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Quicksort:</strong> Pick a pivot, partition the list (smaller left, bigger right), then recurse on both sides.</li>
          <li><strong>Merge Sort:</strong> Split the list in half recursively until size 1, then merge them back in order.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Recursion"
    definition: "A programming technique where a function calls itself to solve a problem."
  - term: "Base Case"
    definition: "The condition that stops the recursion (prevents an infinite loop)."
  - term: "Recursive Step"
    definition: "The part of the function where it calls itself with a modified (usually smaller) parameter."
  - term: "Call Stack"
    definition: "A memory structure that stores 'Stack Frames' for each active function call (tracks local logic)."
  - term: "Stack Overflow"
    definition: "A fatal error that occurs when the Call Stack runs out of memory (usually due to a missing Base Case)."
  - term: "Stack Frame"
    definition: "A section of the stack dedicated to a single function call, storing its arguments, return address, and local variables."
  - term: "Pivot"
    definition: "The element in Quicksort used to partition the array."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Recursion is just a Loop"
    correction: "It feels like a loop, but it eats memory! Each 'loop' adds a new frame to the Stack. An infinite recursion crashes execution; an infinite loop just hangs."
  - mistake: "The Function Stops at Base Case"
    correction: "No! That's just the half-way point. The function must then 'Unwind' all the way back up the stack to finish."
  - mistake: "Variables are Shared"
    correction: "Thinking 'n' is the same variable in every call. Each recursive call has its own separate copy of 'n'."

